## Method and reasoning
Binary-search the first true index in [0,n], where n acts as the end sentinel. For the lower bound use a[i] ≥ x and for the upper bound use a[i] > x. The matching range is [lower,upper). If it is nonempty, the first 1-based index is lower+1 and the last is upper.

## Walk through the sample
In the sample, the run of 1s occupies positions 2 through 4. Its first greater element is at position 5. Their difference, 5 − 2, gives three copies. Searching for 2 instead produces coincident boundaries.

## Complexity
Reading and storing the sorted array costs O(n) time and space. Two boundary searches per query give O(n + q log(n+1)) total time.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int bound(const vector<int> &a, int x, bool upper) {
    int l = 0, r = a.size();
    while (l < r) {
        int mid = l + (r - l) / 2;
        if (a[mid] < x || (upper && a[mid] == x))
            l = mid + 1;
        else
            r = mid;
    }
    return l;
}
int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, q;
    cin >> n >> q;
    vector<int> a(n);
    for (int &x : a)
        cin >> x;
    while (q--) {
        string type;
        int x;
        cin >> type >> x;
        int lo = bound(a, x, false), hi = bound(a, x, true);
        if (type == "COUNT")
            cout << hi - lo;
        else if (lo == hi)
            cout << 0;
        else
            cout << (type == "FIRST" ? lo + 1 : hi);
        cout << '\n';
    }
}
